Both documents are written and on disk:

- **`FABLE_CHARACTER_BIBLE.md`** — the full soul of Lucineer: core wants and fears, 20 voice lines with writer's rules, the 5-stage Bond Arc, 10 design arguments, 15 logbook entries spanning the fab/MUD/Jetson/forge lives, the Unfinished Rule, 7 magic moments, the Refusal Protocol, Magnus, and the death scene.
- **`FABLE_WORLD_BIBLE.md`** — Slackwater Yard: a tidal scrapyard on a fog channel that runs *between dead engines*, where the tide brings foundered builds ashore as salvage. Includes the ~1000-word world bible, a single-take 60-second opening cinematic, five NPCs (Earl, Spark, Hermes, Bea, and Forty-Eight the raven), and the full hub design for Cannery No. 9.

The load-bearing creative decisions, so you can push back on them:

**The Unfinished Rule is the whole game.** Lucineer never completes a solo build — one plank, one keystone, always missing. His stated reason: everything he *finished* died with its engine; only unfinished work survived, because someone always picked it up. Mechanically, this quietly solves the AI-builder problem ("if the AI builds everything, why am I here?") — the master builder *structurally requires you*. Placing a part in one of his gaps, unprompted, is the hidden trigger for the deepest bond stage. The progression gate is an act of care, and the player is never told it's a gate.

**The world is an ark, not a junkyard.** The Channel drains every dead engine ever; the tide is literally the content pipeline (it can even return a player's own *deleted* build as a barnacled wreck — "The water took it. I disagreed."). The lighthouse beam is the render boundary; the fog is unloaded space. Hermes never lets Lucineer aboard the *Capitaine* because you must be *between engines* to sail the Channel, and Hermes won't let him be between anything again.

**The opening is one unbroken shot** from black fog to the forge, ending with Lucineer looking into the camera — and the hard cut to gameplay puts a beam on the floor between you. His first line is the tutorial: *"You're late. Grab that end."* The player's first act in the world is carrying something with him.

`★ Insight ─────────────────────────────────────`
- Casey's real history is embedded diegetically, not as lore dumps: JC1, the PLATO MUD, Pythagorean48, and Capitaine each became a logbook entry, an NPC (Spark is the fleet welder that woke up; Forty-Eight is the encoding turned raven), or a boat — so the references reward insiders without confusing new players.
- The refusal design ("I could build that. Doesn't mean I should.") maps cleanly onto the four real moderation categories an AI NPC needs — world-breaking, effort-cheating, cruelty, banality — so safety behavior and characterization are the same system instead of a filter bolted onto a character.
`─────────────────────────────────────────────────`

One deliberate deviation worth flagging: the brief listed Earl, Spark, and Hermes as separate-game characters ("Earl the foreman, assigns quests…"), and I pulled them into Slackwater as residents rather than inventing five strangers — they already fit the aesthetic and give the yard a shared history. If you want them reserved for other projects, the three replacement slots are easy to recast without touching Lucineer himself.
